<main id="main">
<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/documents.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/documents-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/documents.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="601" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="shell sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span><span aria-current="page">Glossary</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Terms Glossary</h1><p class="lede">41 terms from policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English and searchable.</p></div></section><div class="shell two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="g-search"><label for="gloss-q">Search 41 terms</label><input id="gloss-q" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="g-count" aria-live="polite"></p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="g-item" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Often the first payment under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for extra costs of living elsewhere while a damaged home is repaired, sometimes called loss of use.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of covered damage. This is the only question an appraisal panel decides.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition allowing either party to demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by at least two of the three panel members setting the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>The person each side selects to value the loss and work with the other appraiser and umpire.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-B"><dt>Bad faith</dt><dd>A general term for an insurer’s unreasonable handling of a claim. In Utah it is usually analyzed under the implied covenant of good faith in the policy; a legal question for an attorney.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-C"><dt>Coinsurance</dt><dd>A commercial policy condition that can reduce a claim payment if the property is insured for less than a required percentage of its value.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage on a homeowners policy, the limit used to calculate percentage deductibles.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage, such as detached garages, sheds and fences.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property, or contents, coverage.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-D"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing limits, deductibles, endorsements and the policy period.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value due to age, wear and condition, subtracted from replacement cost to find ACV.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for the cost of removing damaged property and debris after a covered loss, often subject to limits.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-E"><dt>Earthquake endorsement</dt><dd>An addition to a property policy that covers earthquake damage, typically with a percentage deductible.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Endorsement</dt><dd>A written change to a policy that adds, removes or modifies coverage.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Estimate review</dt><dd>A line-by-line audit of an insurance estimate to identify missing items and pricing gaps.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Extended replacement cost</dt><dd>An endorsement that pays above the dwelling limit, by a set percentage, if rebuilding costs more than expected.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-F"><dt>Fair rental value</dt><dd>Coverage for lost rent when a rental property can’t be occupied because of a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>First-party claim</dt><dd>A claim you make under your own insurance policy, as opposed to a claim against someone else’s policy.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-H"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>Recoverable depreciation withheld until repairs are completed, also called recoverable depreciation.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-I"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at a roof’s edge that prevents melting snow from draining and can force water under shingles.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Impartial</dt><dd>Without bias toward either side. Many appraisal clauses require appraisers and umpires to be impartial or disinterested.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-L"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate describing a task or material, its quantity and its price.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs match in color, texture and appearance.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Steps taken to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or water extraction. Policies generally require it.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-O"><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs of meeting current building codes when repairing or rebuilding.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge, often 10% and 10%, for coordinating multiple trades on a repair.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-P"><dt>Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible calculated as a percentage of the dwelling limit rather than a flat dollar amount.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which an insurer may require within a set time after requesting it.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A licensed professional who represents a policyholder in presenting and negotiating a claim for a fee.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation that can be recovered after repairs are completed under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deduction for depreciation.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The full list of damaged items and repairs needed, before pricing.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Snow load</dt><dd>The weight of snow and ice on a roof, measured in pounds per square foot.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Square (roofing)</dt><dd>100 square feet of roof surface, the standard unit for roofing estimates.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>An added labor charge for working on roofs above a certain pitch, often 7/12 or 8/12.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add missing items or costs to an insurance estimate after the initial settlement.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Surplus lines</dt><dd>Insurance written by carriers not licensed as standard insurers in a state, often for hard-to-place risks.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-W"><dt>Wildland-urban interface (WUI)</dt><dd>Areas where homes meet or mix with undeveloped wildland vegetation, raising wildfire risk.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="aside"><div class="aside-in"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-t">Talk it through, free</h2><p>Send the carrier’s estimate or call. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal is worth it.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-accent btn-block" href="/contact-utah-appraiser/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-t">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/how-utah-appraisal-works/">How appraisal works<svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/utah-appraisal-faq/">Appraisal FAQ<svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/claim-terms-glossary/" aria-current="page">Claim terms glossary<svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/utah-claim-articles/">Claim articles<svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div>
